---
import PageNav from "./PageNav.astro";
import TeachConcept from "./TeachConcept.astro";
import TeachFacts from "./TeachFacts.astro";
import { smallestLineSpec } from "../data/teach/line";
import { playgroundHref, specText } from "../data/teach/spec";

const href = "/docs/charts/line";
const spec = specText(smallestLineSpec);
const play = playgroundHref(smallestLineSpec);
const curve = `{
  "mark": { "curve": "linear" }
}`;
const step = `{
  "mark": { "curve": "step" }
}`;
const points = `{
  "mark": { "points": "always" }
}`;
const area = `{
  "mark": { "area": true }
}`;
const channelArea = `{
  "encoding": {
    "y": [
      { "field": "revenue", "title": "Revenue", "area": true },
      { "field": "target", "title": "Target", "dash": "dashed" }
    ]
  }
}`;
const legend = `{
  "legend": { "position": "top" }
}`;
const tooltipOff = `{
  "tooltip": false
}`;
const tooltipOn = `{
  "tooltip": true
}`;
---

<p>
  A line shows how a measure changes across an ordered x. Each row is one position on that x. A second measure is a second line in the same chart.
</p>

<section id="when-to-use">
  <h2>When to use it</h2>
  <p>
    Use a line for a trend: time, or any categories that have an order. A bar is the chart for comparing totals that do not form a sequence. A pie or a donut is the chart for parts of one total. A sparkline is the small line with no axes, for a table cell or a KPI.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="area">
  <h2>Line and area</h2>
  <p>
    There is no <code>type: "area"</code>. A filled line is still <code>type: "line"</code>, with <code>mark.area</code> set to true. <a href="/docs/charts/area">Area</a> is the page for that note.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>mark.area</code> fills under every series. A single y channel can set <code>area: true</code> and leave the others as lines. Revenue against target fills revenue and draws the target as a dashed line.
  </p>
  <p>
    Several series that add up to one shape are a <a href="/docs/charts/stacked-area">stacked area</a>. That chart has its own type.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="data">
  <h2>Data</h2>
  <p>
    Each row is one x and one or more numbers. <code>encoding.x</code> is the category or the time. <code>encoding.y</code> is a field name, or a list of channels when the chart has more than one series.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quarterly units is one series, <code>units</code>, across four quarters. Revenue against target is two series, <code>revenue</code> and <code>target</code>, across twelve months. Revenue and cost is two series across six months.
  </p>
  <p>
    A missing number leaves a gap in that series, unless <code>mark.connectNulls</code> is true. <code>a11y.alt</code> is required. One sentence, 10 to 500 characters, with the point of the chart.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="spec">
  <h2>A spec that renders</h2>
  <p>
    This is the smallest line. <code>version</code>, <code>type</code>, and <code>a11y.alt</code> are required. The curve, the points, and the fill come from the line defaults, so <code>mark</code> can be omitted. The default curve is monotone. Points show on hover. The area is off.
  </p>
  <TeachFacts type="line" label="Line packages" spec={spec} />
</section>

<p>Each option below is a key on the spec. The demo changes that key and updates the chart and the snippet together.</p>

<section id="examples">
  <h2>Examples</h2>
  <p>
    The rail switches among existing line specs. Quarterly units is one series. Revenue against target is two series, with a fill on revenue only. Deploys, step curve holds each value until the next day. Revenue and cost names the tooltip field. The <a href="/docs/charts/area">area</a> note and <a href="/docs/charts/stacked-area">stacked area</a> are the filled cousins.
  </p>
</section>

<TeachConcept id="curve" title="Curve">
  <p>
    <code>mark.curve</code> is how the line joins the points. <code>monotone</code> is the default. It stays inside the data, so a spike does not overshoot. <code>linear</code> draws straight segments. <code>step</code> holds the value and then jumps, which is the right curve for a count that changes and then sits still.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.curve</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{curve}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.curve step</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{step}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <code>step-before</code> and <code>step-after</code> place the jump on the other side of the point. Set those in the playground.
  </p>
</TeachConcept>

<TeachConcept id="points" title="Points">
  <p>
    <code>mark.points</code> is <code>none</code>, <code>hover</code>, or <code>always</code>. The default is <code>hover</code>: the dot appears under the pointer. <code>always</code> draws a dot on every point. <code>none</code> draws the line only. The field is <code>mark.points</code>, not <code>mark.markers</code>.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.points</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{points}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</TeachConcept>

<TeachConcept id="area-fill" title="Area fill">
  <p>
    <code>mark.area</code> fills under every line. Omit it, or set false, and the chart is a line. Set true and the same spec is a filled line. That is the chart at <a href="/docs/charts/area">/docs/charts/area</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.area</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{area}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <p>
    To fill one series and leave another as a line, set <code>area: true</code> on that y channel. The Area fill control writes <code>mark.area</code> for every series. A mixed spec, such as Revenue against target, leaves both buttons off until you choose one.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>encoding.y area</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{channelArea}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</TeachConcept>

<TeachConcept id="legend" title="Legend">
  <p>
    A line with one series hides the legend. A line with several series shows it. Omit <code>legend</code>, or set <code>true</code>, and the legend sits on top. <code>false</code> hides it. <code>legend.position</code> is <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, <code>left</code>, or <code>inside</code>.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>legend.position</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{legend}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</TeachConcept>

<TeachConcept id="tooltip" title="Tooltip">
  <p>
    <code>tooltip</code> is on for a line when the key is omitted. Hovering a point shows the series and the value. <code>tooltip: false</code> turns the card off. <code>tooltip: true</code> turns it on.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>tooltip false</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{tooltipOff}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>tooltip true</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{tooltipOn}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <code>tooltip.fields</code> chooses the rows in the card, and <code>tooltip.position</code> places it. Revenue and cost sets the fields. Change those in the playground. Turning the card off and on again puts that object back.
  </p>
</TeachConcept>

<section id="more">
  <h2>The rest of the spec</h2>
  <p>
    <code>axes.x.title</code> and <code>axes.y.title</code> name the axes. <code>mark.sampling</code> downsamples a long line. <code>mark.strokeWidth</code>, <code>mark.pointShape</code>, and <code>mark.labelFormat</code> refine the drawing. Theme presets live on the playground, with the full option list.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="related">
  <h2>Related charts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/area">Area</a> is this chart with <code>mark.area</code>. The spec type is still <code>line</code>.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/stacked-area">Stacked area</a> adds the series into one shape.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/bar">Bar</a> compares categories that are not a trend.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/sparkline">Sparkline</a> is a line with no axes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href={play}>Open this spec in the playground</a>. That link opens the spec on this page. The demo link opens the chart you are looking at, including any option you change.
  </p>
</section>

<PageNav href={href} />
